---
term: Limit of arbitration
section: premise
order: 40
definition: The observation that some conflicts have no shared interest above them that can settle them, and that some groups are too alien to include in any common charter.
seeAlso:
  - arbitration
  - highest-human-charter
  - natural-charter
---
Arbitration needs a parent whose [[higher-order common interest]] covers every party. Many conflicts have one. For example, two fishing cooperatives that disagree about quotas both want the fishery to survive. Conflicts over values or identity often have no such interest. Some groups, such as a tribe that hunts people for food, reject the existence of others outright.

The Social Charter handles these cases with a second mode of resolution. Where no shared charter covers a conflict, the parties settle it themselves through *noncooperative struggle*, such as competition, persuasion, recruitment, and exit, within the bounds that their common charters set.

The same reasoning sets the boundary of the [[highest human charter]]. A charter that included every human would share no real interest, which would make it no different from the [[Natural Charter]]. So the highest human charter is the largest coalition that shares a real interest, and the agreement of that coalition decides whom it includes. Groups outside it relate to it through struggle under the Natural Charter.
